# Attack-path analysis
Final: ignore | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: ignore | Policy adjusted: ignore
## Rationale
The finding is a valid functional regression but not a security vulnerability. Static evidence and validation both show that the new frontend buttons call the backend OpenURL path, and the backend allow-list rejects the new Google and keys.openpgp.org hosts. This restrictive failure mode prevents navigation rather than enabling arbitrary URL opening. There is no public exposure, no attacker-controlled input, no privilege or identity change, no secret disclosure, and no cross-boundary effect. Probability of security exploitation is therefore effectively none, and security impact is none; adjusted criticality is ignore.
## Likelihood
ignore - The UI regression is easy for a user to trigger, but there is no meaningful attacker exploitation path. The hard-coded URLs are not attacker-controlled and the backend rejects them before opening a browser.
## Impact
ignore - The impact is limited to diagnostic guidance buttons failing to open in the affected user's local desktop UI. The behavior does not expose secrets, does not broaden network access, does not allow attacker-controlled URL opening, and does not execute code.
## Assumptions
- The analyzed repository checkout is the complete product source for the affected commit.
- The Wails desktop application is the relevant runtime deployment; no server, cloud deployment, Kubernetes, or load balancer artifacts are present in the repository checkout.
- The provided validation evidence accurately reflects the same commit and normal backend code path used by the frontend Wails binding.
- A user runs the affected desktop application build
- The user opens the diagnostic report guidance UI
- The user clicks one of the newly added diagnostic guidance link buttons
## Path
User click -> Vue openURL -> Wails api.OpenURL -> App.OpenURL -> trustedExternalURL allow-list -> rejection/error
## Path evidence
- `src/frontend/src/main.js:1251-1253` - The frontend openURL method logs the action and directly calls the Wails backend api.OpenURL with the supplied URL.
- `src/frontend/src/main.js:2174-2177` - The diagnostic guidance includes a new inline button that calls openURL with a hard-coded https://www.google.com/search URL.
- `src/frontend/src/main.js:2195-2198` - The diagnostic guidance includes a new inline button that calls openURL with a hard-coded https://keys.openpgp.org/search URL.
- `src/app.go:408-413` - App.OpenURL validates the supplied URL with trustedExternalURL before invoking runtime.BrowserOpenURL.
- `src/app.go:423-433` - trustedExternalURL requires HTTPS and only allows github.com, hatolife.booth.pm, support.discord.com, and x.com, so the two new hosts are rejected.
- `src/wails.json:1-12` - The repository configuration identifies this as a Wails desktop application; only a localhost frontend dev server is configured for development, not a production public network service.
## Narrative
The reported behavior is real and reachable in the main desktop application: two new diagnostic guidance buttons call openURL with www.google.com and keys.openpgp.org, and the frontend forwards those URLs to the Wails backend. The backend then validates with trustedExternalURL before calling BrowserOpenURL. Because the allow-list contains only github.com, hatolife.booth.pm, support.discord.com, and x.com, the new links are rejected. This causes broken UI links, but the security control remains restrictive. There is no attacker-controlled input, no inbound exposure, no secret access, no arbitrary URL opening, and no cross-boundary impact. The appropriate security criticality is ignore.
## Controls
- trustedExternalURL enforces HTTPS-only external browser targets
- trustedExternalURL uses exact hostname allow-listing
- App.OpenURL validates before BrowserOpenURL
- New hard-coded URLs are blocked rather than opened
- No public ingress, load balancer, or service port artifacts found in the repository checkout
## Blindspots
- Static review did not run the full Wails GUI, though the backend path was validated by the provided executable test evidence.
- Operating-system-specific BrowserOpenURL behavior was not independently tested because the relevant URLs are rejected before that sink.
- Repository-only analysis cannot prove behavior of unofficial downstream builds that modify the allow-list.